                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} String Theory
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory+and+physics"} number theory and physics
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis+and+physics"} Riemann hypothesis and physics
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#applications"} Applications
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesBFCGTheory"} BFCG theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %strong BF-theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            defined by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi BF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{BF}
            on a space of certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-manifold"} 4-manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            is given by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra-valued+1-forms"} Lie algebra-valued 1-forms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;A
                with values in some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_1
                and with
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                2-form
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_A
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-forms"} differential 2-forms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;B
                with values in some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔤
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_2
            %li
              %p together with
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    a homomorphism
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∂
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mn 2
                          %mo →
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \colon \mathfrak{g}_2 \to \mathfrak{g}_1
                %li
                  %p
                    an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,- \rangle
          %p by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi BF
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{BF}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  (A,B) \;\mapsto\;
                  \\int_X \langle F_A \wedge \partial B\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            There is not much of a proposal in the literature for how to make sense of this expression globally. It has been observed that it looks like the action functional is one on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra-valued+forms"} ∞-Lie algebra-valued forms
            with values in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+Lie+2-algebra"} strict Lie 2-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∂
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = (\mathfrak{g}_2 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} \mathfrak{g}_1)
          %p
            This would suggest that the BF-action functional is to be regarded as a functional on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"}> 2-groupoid
            ) of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundles"} principal 2-bundles
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+2-bundle"} connection on a 2-bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = (G_2 \to G_1)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-group"} Lie 2-group
            integrating
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            If one couples to the above action functional that for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            with coefficients as in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_X( \langle F_A \wedge B\rangle - \frac{1}{2} \langle F_A \wedge F_A\rangle - \frac{1}{2}\langle \partial B \wedge \partial B\rangle)
          %p
            then this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Chern-Simons+theory"} generalized Chern-Simons theory
            action functional induced from the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+Lie+2-algebra"} strict Lie 2-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
            for details.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            Much of the interest in BF-theory results from the fact that on a 4-dimensional manifold, to some extent the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            may be encoded in BF-theory form. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity+as+a+BF-theory"} gravity as a BF-theory
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p BF theory was maybe first considered in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gary+Horowitz"}> Gary Horowitz
              ,
              %em Exactly soluable diffeomorphism invariant theories
              Commun. Math. Phys. 125, 417-437 (1989)
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              Danny Birmingham,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Blau"} Matthias Blau
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Thompson"} George Thompson
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological field theory
              %strong 209
              4–5 (1991) 129-340 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-1573(91)90117-5"}> doi:10.1016/0370-1573(91)90117-5
              ]
          %p
            The observation that the BF-theory action functional looks like it should be read as a functional on a space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebra+valued+forms"} ∞-Lie algebra valued forms
            with values in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+Lie+2-algebra"} strict Lie 2-algebra
            possibly appears in print first in
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/hep-th/pdf/0309/0309173v2.pdf#page=22"} section 3.9
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Florian+Girelli"}> Florian Girelli
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hendryk+Pfeiffer"} Hendryk Pfeiffer
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher gauge theory – differential versus integral formulation
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0309173"} arXiv:hep-th/0309173
          %p
            The observation that coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
            it can be read as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            action functional on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+2-bundles"} connections on 2-bundles
            is in
          %ul#SSSI
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                \-conections
              (
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+(%E2%88%9E%2C1)-topos+--+references#SSSI"}> web
              )
          %p and a more comprehensive discussion is in section 4.3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
              \.
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Aristide Baratin,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Florian+Girelli"} Florian Girelli
              = succeed "," do
                %em Diffeomorphisms in group field theories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.83.104051"} doi
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0590"} arxiv/1101.0590
          %p
            Relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            (in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Mariano Celada, Diego González, Merced Montesinos,
                %em BF gravity
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02020"}> arxiv/1610.02020
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+S.+Cattaneo"}> Alberto S. Cattaneo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leon+Menger"} Leon Menger
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michele+Schiavina"} Michele Schiavina
                %em Gravity with torsion as deformed BF theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01877"}> arXiv:2310.01877
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On a version of BF-theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"} arithmetic
            related to non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientability
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+schemes"} arithmetic schemes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Magnus+Carlson"}> Magnus Carlson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minhyong+Kim"} Minhyong Kim
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note on abelian arithmetic BF-theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02236"} arXiv:1911.02236
          %p
            Via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+S.+Cattaneo"}> Alberto S. Cattaneo
              \:
              %em Surface Observables, 2-Knot Invariants, and Nonabelian Electric Fluxes
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13623"}> arXiv:2511.13623
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesBFCGTheory BFCG theory
          %p There is a more general BFCG action introduced by Girelli, Pfeiffer and Popescu which has been shown to be a special case of the categorified BF-theory in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jo%C3%A3o+Faria+Martins"}> João Faria Martins
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleksandar+Mikovi%C4%87"} Aleksandar Miković
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lie crossed modules and gauge-invariant actions for 2-BF theories
              %strong 15
              4 (2011) 913-1199 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.0903"}> arXiv:1006.0903
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.atmp/1339438351"} euclid:atmp/1339438351
          %p
            Discussion of BFCG theory via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections+on+2-bundles"} connections on 2-bundles
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
          %ul
            %li
              A.D. López-Hernández, Graciela Reyes-Ahumada, Javier Chagoya:
              %em Categorical generalization of BF theory coupled to gravity
              [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.02889"}> arXiv:2408.02889
              ]
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on November 18, 2025 at 07:07:43.
            See the
            %a{:href => "/nlab/history/BF-theory", :style => "color: #005c19"} history
            of this page for a list of all contributions to it.
